   * - Ask once for everything it will answer at once: change all the candidates, run it once, and read which ones it names back. A per-item loop is the fallback.
   * - Prefer looping in the program rather than in the command: the values stay in variables you can use in the next call and return at the end, and a mistake stops at a line instead of somewhere inside a heredoc. When a script really is the right tool — a parser, something with no JS equivalent — write the script.
   *
   * @example const r = await tools.bash("make check 2>&1");
   */
  function bash(command: string): Promise<{ status: number; stdout: string; stderr: string; truncated?: boolean }>;

  /**
   * Create a new file atomically. Errors if the path exists — for an existing file use `replace_file`.
   *
   * @example await tools.create_file("notes.md", body);
   */
  function create_file(path: string, content: string): Promise<{ version: string }>;

